Indeed. The successful attempts to score MAXINT-1
points were exercises of detailed game knowledge
and skill. That doesn't make every point scored in
an ascension by anyone equally meritorious.

The first time reaching MAXINT-1 was done in public,
by eit-brad in the November tournament a few years
back, it was done with the expressed purpose of
proving that NetHack's scoring is broken, broken,
broken. That goal was universally recognized to have
been accomplished.

It is surely not the case that eit-brad is
1000-fold better a NetHack player than you.

What is the case is that it is possible to drive up
the score, by more NetHack mechanisms than can
possibly be countered, so that "your ascensions"
compared to "someone else's ascensions" can't really
be compared meaningfully based on scoretable scores.

It is not _necessarily_ possible to fix the scoring
in NetHack, especially scoring for ascensions, too
many things are considered "skilled play" to bring
them all into alignment in a single scoring system,
but it is at least, maybe, worth an attempt to mend
the scoring to be not quite as tremendously awful as
it currently is.

Quantum valeat.

